why 36 0 SEVENTH In the final V⁷ the tenor's A♭, the seventh, falls by step to G.
why 4 1/2 SEVENTH The bass's B♭ is the dominant note, with the soprano's C held over it, not a seventh. The bass then plays the cadence formula B♭–F–B♭ into the half cadence.
why 4 1/2 LEAPS The tenor descends from E♭ through B♭ to A, the leading tone of B♭. The tritone E♭–A is spread over several notes and two chords.
why 5 0 LEADING_TONE The tenor's A, leading tone of B♭, leaps up to D, the third of the chord at the fermata. Rising to B♭ would leave the chord without its third.
why 8 1/2 OVERLAP The bass leaps up an octave on the same E♭ chord and meets the tenor in unison, as the whole texture climbs with the melody to its high G.
why 8 1/2 RANGE The octave leap takes the bass up to a high E♭ to keep the chord close under the climbing tune. It walks back down by step over the next bars.
why 8 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices rise with the melody's climb E♭–F–G. The bass only changes octave within the same chord, so the lines keep their independence.
why 9 0 RANGE Still high: the bass steps down E♭–D–C–B♭–A from its octave leap, back to its usual range.
why 9 3/4 LEAPS The bass walks down E♭–D–C–B♭–A by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 14 0 DOUBLING The tenor's D♭, a seventh, must fall to C, and the tune rises B♭–C. Both reach C, so the third is doubled: a correct resolution beats the ideal doubling.
why 15 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls into V⁷ as the phrase closes: soprano G–F, alto E♭–D, the tenor's B♭–A♭ and the bass to B♭.
why 15 1/2 LEAPS The bass falls C–F–B♭: roots moving by fifths (vi–ii–V), the strongest bass motion, ending low for the cadence.
why 19 0 CROSSING The alto leaps down from F to B♭, under the tenor's E♭, and climbs back C–D. The tenor holds the tonic while the alto finds room below it.
why 19 1/4 CROSSING The alto climbs back B♭–C–D past the tenor's held E♭. By the next beat it is above again.
why 20 0 LEADING_TONE A deceptive cadence to C minor (vi). The soprano takes E♭, so the alto's D falls to C and doubles the root of vi.
why 21 1/2 DOUBLING The new phrase starts on V⁶ with the leading tone D in alto and bass. The alto moves straight on, D–E♭–F, so the doubling lasts one eighth.
why 23 0 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto strikes F again over the E♭ chord and falls to E♭: a suspension struck anew instead of tied, resolving down by step.
why 27 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Tenor and bass move from C/F to B♭/E♭ a fifth apart, with the tenor's passing A♭ in between. In this final cadence the passing note breaks them up for Bach; strict exercises still count them.
why 28 1/2 RANGE The new phrase starts with the bass up at E♭, in unison with the tenor, and it falls by step to A over the next bar.
why 32 0 MELODIC At the fermata the bass drops a seventh to C instead of stepping up to C, so the deceptive vi sounds low and full. It is the same note an octave lower.
why 34 0 LEAPS The bass falls G–E♭–A♭, outlining the E♭ chord before landing on the root of IV. Leaping through chord notes keeps the line clear.
why 34 1/2 OVERLAP The tune leaps down from C to G, below where the alto just was. The alto falls to E♭ at the same moment, so the voices stay in order.
why 35 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The same cadence as in bar 15: every voice falls into V⁷, the bass from F to B♭.
